%I A400711 #27 Nov 01 2026 23:14:52
%S A400711 1,8,3,10,5,12,7,14,9,64,11,66,13,68,15,70,17,24,19,26,21,28,23,30,25,
%T A400711 80,27,82,29,84,31,86,33,40,35,42,37,44,39,46,41,96,43,98,45,100,47,
%U A400711 102,49,56,51,58,53,60,55,62,57,112,59,114,61,116,63,118,65,72,67,74,69,76,71,78,73,512,75,514,77,516,79,518,81,88,83,90
%N A400711 Row n = 1 of the array in A400710.
%C A400711 a(n) is the sum of n and 1 using the addition rule defined in A400710.
%C A400711 Let b = (b(0), b(1), b(2), ...) denote the binary expansion of n, where b(0) is the least significant bit, padded with infinitely many extra zeros on the right.
%C A400711 Let k >= 0 be the smallest number such that b(3*k) = 0.
%C A400711 Then a(n) = n + 8^k - Sum_{i=0..k-1} 8^i.
%C A400711 For example, if n is even, k=0, and a(n) = n+1.
%C A400711 If k=1, as it is for n = 1, 3, 5, and 7, a(n) = n+7.
%C A400711 If k=2, as it is for n = 9, 11, 13, and 15, a(n) = n+55.
%C A400711 And so on.
%H A400711 Michael S. Branicky, <a href="/A400711/b400711.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%o A400711 (Python)
%o A400711 def T(n, k):
%o A400711     if n == 0: return k
%o A400711     if k == 0: return n
%o A400711     return T(n^k, (n&k)<<3)
%o A400711 def a(n): return T(n, 1)
%o A400711 print([a(n) for n in range(84)]) # _Michael S. Branicky_, Nov 01 2026
%Y A400711 Cf. A400710, A400712.
%K A400711 nonn,new
%O A400711 0,2
%A A400711 _N. J. A. Sloane_, Oct 29 2026
